I can create databases. Now let me test the plpgsql behaviors in r3test:

Test 1: COMMIT in a plain procedure (no exception clause) — CREATE + CALL.
Test 2: COMMIT inside a block with an EXCEPTION clause — CREATE + CALL. Does CREATE fail? Does CALL fail?
Test 3: ROLLBACK inside an EXCEPTION handler.
Test 4: `SELECT COUNT(*) INTO v;` without FROM.
Test 5: Composite variable `v_row monthly_report;` — well, needs a table; skip standalone, test with real tables later.

Let me write a test SQL file to /tmp/opencode and run it with ON_ERROR_STOP=0 so I can see all results at once. Actually, since errors abort... let me run separate psql invocations so each test is independent.

Test file approach — I'll create /tmp/opencode/t1.sql with:

```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE t_commit_plain() LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
BEGIN
    NULL;
    COMMIT;
END;
$$;

CALL t_commit_plain();

CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE t_commit_exc() LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
BEGIN
    NULL;
    COMMIT;
EXCEPTION WHEN OTHERS THEN
    RAISE;
END;
$$;

CALL t_commit_exc();
```

If CREATE t_commit_exc fails at compile time, then ON_ERROR_STOP matters. Let me run without ON_ERROR_STOP first to observe each statement's outcome independently, using `-a` (echo all) so I can map outputs to statements.

Also test 3:
```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE t_rollback_exc() LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
BEGIN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'boom';
EXCEPTION WHEN OTHERS THEN
    ROLLBACK;
    RAISE;
END;
$$;
CALL t_rollback_exc();
```

Test 4:
```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE t_count() LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
DECLARE
    v numeric := 0;
BEGIN
    SELECT COUNT(*) INTO v;
    RAISE NOTICE 'v=%', v;
END;
$$;
CALL t_count();
```

Let me write these as separate files and run each separately to isolate errors. Or one file with -a and no ON_ERROR_STOP — psql continues after errors by default. With -a each statement echoed, so I can tell what succeeded. Let me do that in one run for efficiency, plus a second run testing the "CALL inside explicit transaction" scenario.

